To render a list of prime numbers from 1 to 100, and display only the numbers that are greater than 50 and less than 80, you can use a loop with conditional rendering in React. Here's an example of how you can achieve this:

```jsx
import React from 'react';

function PrimeNumbers() {
  const primeNumbers = [];

  for (let i = 1; i <= 100; i++) {
    let isPrime = true;

    // Check if the number is prime
    for (let j = 2; j < i; j++) {
      if (i % j === 0) {
        isPrime = false;
        break;
      }
    }

    if (isPrime && i > 50 && i < 80) {
      primeNumbers.push(i);
    }
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>List of Prime Numbers (greater than 50 and less than 80)</h1>
      <ul>
        {primeNumbers.map((number) => (
          <li key={number}>{number}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
}

export default PrimeNumbers;
```

In this code, we iterate from 1 to 100 and check if each number is prime using a nested loop. If a number is prime and it is greater than 50 and less than 80, we add it to the `primeNumbers` array. Finally, we render the list of prime numbers within an unordered list (`<ul>`) in the React component.

Remember to import and use this `PrimeNumbers` component in your React application to see the rendered list of prime numbers.

