AJAX (Asynchronous JavaScript and XML) long-polling is a technique used to allow real-time communication between the client (browser) and server. It involves the client continuously sending requests to the server, and the server holding the response until new data is available or a timeout occurs.

Here are the steps to implement AJAX long-polling in JavaScript:

1. Create an HTML file:
   Start by creating an HTML file with a button to initiate the long-polling request. Name the file `index.html` and add the following code:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>AJAX Long-Polling</title>
  <script src="script.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
  <button onclick="startLongPolling()">Start Long-Polling</button>
  <div id="response"></div>
</body>
</html>
```

2. Create a JavaScript file:
   Create a new JavaScript file named `script.js` in the same directory as the HTML file and add the following code:

```javascript
// Vanilla JavaScript Implementation
function startLongPolling() {
  // Send a GET request to the server
  const request = new XMLHttpRequest();
  request.open("GET", "/api/data", true);

  // Handle the response
  request.onreadystatechange = function () {
    if (request.readyState === 4 && request.status === 200) {
      document.getElementById("response").innerText = request.responseText;
      startLongPolling(); // Restart the long-polling request
    }
  };

  // Handle errors and timeouts
  request.onerror = function () {
    console.error("An error occurred during the long-polling request.");
    startLongPolling(); // Restart the long-polling request
  };
  request.ontimeout = function () {
    console.error("The long-polling request timed out.");
    startLongPolling(); // Restart the long-polling request
  };

  // Set the request timeout (e.g., 30 seconds)
  request.timeout = 30000;

  // Send the request
  request.send();
}
```

3. Configure the server-side:
   In this tutorial, we assume there is a server-side endpoint `/api/data` that handles the long-polling request and sends back the data when available. You need to configure your server to handle this endpoint and return the response.

That's it for the vanilla JavaScript implementation of AJAX long-polling. Now let's see how to implement it using React.js:

1. Install React.js and set up the project:
   Start by creating a new React.js project using create-react-app.
   Open your terminal and run the following commands:

```bash
npx create-react-app long-polling-demo
cd long-polling-demo
```

2. Update the `App.js` file:
   Open the `src/App.js` file and replace the existing code with the following code:

```jsx
import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

function App() {
  const [response, setResponse] = useState('');

  useEffect(() => {
    startLongPolling();
  }, []);

  const startLongPolling = () => {
    // Send a GET request to the server
    fetch('/api/data')
      .then(response => response.text())
      .then(data => {
        setResponse(data);
        startLongPolling(); // Restart the long-polling request
      })
      .catch(error => {
        console.error('An error occurred during the long-polling request.');
        startLongPolling(); // Restart the long-polling request
      });
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={startLongPolling}>Start Long-Polling</button>
      <div id="response">{response}</div>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

3. Configure the server-side:
   Same as before, you need to configure your server to handle the `/api/data` endpoint and return the response.

With these steps, you have implemented AJAX long-polling using both vanilla JavaScript and React.js. The code continuously sends requests to the server and updates the response in the browser whenever new data is available. Error handling and re-establishment procedures are also implemented to handle disruptions or unsuccessful connections.

Regarding session termination during long polling, you can add a timeout on the server-side. If the server doesn't receive any requests for a certain period of time, it can terminate the session. You can tune the session termination duration based on your application's requirements.

To fine-tune client actions in accordance with server response durations, you can adjust the timeout value in the `XMLHttpRequest` or `fetch` request. For example, you can set a longer timeout if the server takes more time to respond, or set a shorter timeout if you want to be more responsive to server updates.

Remember to modify the server-side code according to your backend technology and requirements.

