Incorporating a back-off strategy is indeed a good approach to minimize unnecessary requests during periods of inactivity or when no new data is available. Back-off strategies are commonly used in real-time applications to alleviate the number of unsuccessful attempts, especially when the server is unresponsive or over-loaded. The basic idea behind the back-off strategy is to increase the wait time between consecutive requests when no data is available.

Let's see how to implement a back-off strategy in the JavaScript and React.js code we utilized previously:

1. Vanilla JavaScript implementation:

In `script.js`,

```javascript
var backoff = 0; // Initialize backoff count

function startLongPolling() {
  const request = new XMLHttpRequest();
  request.open("GET", "/api/data", true);
  ...

  request.onreadystatechange = function () {
    if (request.readyState === 4) {
      if (request.status === 200) {
        // Reset backoff count when data is available
        backoff = 0;
        document.getElementById("response").innerText = request.responseText;

        // Now, rather than instantly restarting long-polling, delay the next request
        setTimeout(startLongPolling, getBackoffTime());
      } else if (request.status === 204) {
        // When the server returns a 204 status (No Content), increase the backoff count and delay the next request
        backoff++;
        setTimeout(startLongPolling, getBackoffTime());
      }
    }
  };
  ...
}

// A simple back-off function that returns a delay based on the back-off count, capped at 60 seconds
function getBackoffTime() {
  return Math.min(backoff * 2000, 60000);
}
```

2. React.js implementation:

In `src/App.js`,

```jsx
var backoff = 0; // Initialize backoff count

const App = () => {
  const [response, setResponse] = useState('');

  useEffect(() => {
    startLongPolling();
  }, []);

  const startLongPolling = () => {
    fetch('/api/data')
      .then(response => {
        if (response.ok) {
          // Reset backoff count when data is available
          backoff = 0;
          return response.text();
        } else if (response.status === 204) {
          // Use a Promise.reject() to trigger the .catch() block when the server returns a 204 status (No Content)
          throw new Error('No data available');
        }
      })
      .then(data => {
        setResponse(data);
        setTimeout(startLongPolling, getBackoffTime()); // Add backoff time before next request
      })
      .catch(() => {
        backoff++;
        setTimeout(startLongPolling, getBackoffTime());
      });
  };

  const getBackoffTime = () => {
    return Math.min(backoff * 2000, 60000);
  }
  ...
}
```

Now the long-polling requests will be spaced out and less frequent when no new data is available, reducing the server's processing load and optimizing network usage. As soon as new data becomes available, the code resets the backoff count to zero and resuming regular polling intervals. The `getBackoffTime()` function calculates the delay time for each subsequent request, and to prevent long waiting periods, the delay time is capped at 60 seconds.

